workbench {"content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The engine contract below was written for its command line. Here: `edit <id> --changes` is its PNG/GIF worker \u00e2\u20ac\u201d send only the `changes` list (no `version` / `source_sha256` envelope; `layer` defaults to \"image\"). `draw` is its animation job \u00e2\u20ac\u201d a `recipe` is a job + scene, `--changes` and `--inspect` replace its edit/inspect decisions, and finish is not needed. Ignore file paths, commands and experiment links; the server fills every path field.\n\n---\n\n# Python pixel editing tools\n\nReusable operations for exact pixel repair, palette correction and local frame copying. The model supplies JSON and reviews images; Python executes the edits. No character-specific code, model connection or Aseprite installation is required.\n\n## Compact operation contract\n\nAll operations require `operation`, `layer`, `frames` and an observed `reason`. Frames are unique **one-based** integers; coordinates are **zero-based** integers; rectangles include both endpoints. Colors are exact `#RRGGBB` or `#RRGGBBAA`; six-digit colors mean opaque. Unknown fields, invalid colors, duplicate assignments, missing layers and invalid bounds fail validation.\n\n| Operation | Additional fields | Behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `patch_pixels` | `pixels: [[x,y,expected,replacement], ...]` | Assign only if every selected pixel has its expected RGBA value. Allows deliberate alpha/coverage repair. A mismatch fails the pass. |\n| `map_colors` | `region`, `colors: {\"#from\":\"#to\", ...}` | Simultaneous exact mapping within selected layers/regions/frames. A\u00e2\u2020\u2019B and B\u00e2\u2020\u2019C leaves original A pixels as B. Alpha must stay equal. Unmatched entries change nothing; inspect audit counts. |\n| `copy_region` | `source_layer`, `source_frame`, `region`, `at: [x,y]` | Replace a rectangle, **including transparency**, using the input snapshot before the raster-operation list. Verify alignment and intended motion before copying. |\n| `outline` | `mode`, mode-specific `color` / `colors`, optional width and silhouette settings | Add outer/inner strokes, recolor selected edge colors or erase an edge band. See the [outline contract](OUTLINE_TOOLS.md). |\n| `select_mask` | `name`, `selector`, optional `combine`, `offsets` | Save a reusable color/region/component/alpha selection without changing artwork. See [paint tools](PAINT_TOOLS.md). |\n| `flood_fill` | `seed`, `expected`, `color`, optional matching/boundary settings | Guarded connected-area fill, constrained by its region and optional mask. |\n| `brush` | `points`, paint `color`, optional `size`, `shape`, `pixel_perfect`, `mode`, `region` | Continuous sized brush or eraser; single-point stamps also work. |\n\nEvery mutating raster operation accepts optional `mask: \"name\"` to constrain it to a prior `select_mask`. Read the [paint contract](PAINT_TOOLS.md) for selector fields, lifetime, alpha matching and brush semantics. Selection previews and numeric masks are included in worker outputs for inspection.\n\nOperations run in list order. Pixel patches can expect colors produced by an earlier mapping. Copying always reads the initial reference snapshot, even if another operation edits that source frame. Python input arrays are never mutated, including on failure.\n\nOutline geometry also reads the initial snapshot. Use a union of body layers to avoid borders between parts, excluding shadows/effects when separate layers are available. Inspect the changed silhouette; outline styling is not an automatic cleanup filter.\n\nExample operations (illustrative coordinates/colors, not a preset):\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"operation\":\"map_colors\",\"layer\":\"image\",\"frames\":[2,3],\"region\":[10,12,20,24],\"colors\":{\"#9a7352\":\"#916d4e\",\"#70543c\":\"#684e38\"},\"reason\":\"Two cloth shades drift in frames 2 and 3.\"},\n  {\"operation\":\"patch_pixels\",\"layer\":\"image\",\"frames\":[3],\"pixels\":[[15,18,\"#916d4e\",\"#684e38\"]],\"reason\":\"One highlight interrupts the reviewed shadow cluster.\"},\n  {\"operation\":\"copy_region\",\"layer\":\"image\",\"frames\":[3],\"source_layer\":\"image\",\"source_frame\":2,\"region\":[12,14,13,15],\"at\":[12,14],\"reason\":\"The inspected stationary detail is missing in frame 3.\"}\n]\n```\n\n## Recipe: recolour part of a sprite into a new material\n\nFor clothing, armour or a colour variant that stays inside the outline, recolour a selection instead of drawing pixels: the shading carries over and the outline cannot move.\n\n1. `inspect` the part in every frame to find its edges and how it moves.\n2. `select_mask` it as a `polygon`; `offsets` (one `[dx,dy]` per frame) let one shape follow a part that only moves. Give a frame its own polygon where the part changes shape.\n3. `map_colors` with that `mask`: old ramp to new ramp, shade for shade.\n\nIllustrative values, not a preset:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"operation\":\"select_mask\",\"layer\":\"image\",\"frames\":[1,2,3],\"name\":\"part\",\"selector\":{\"kind\":\"polygon\",\"points\":[[10,12],[16,12],[16,20],[10,20]]},\"offsets\":[[0,0],[0,-1],[0,-1]],\"reason\":\"The part, 1 px higher in frames 2-3.\"},\n  {\"operation\":\"map_colors\",\"layer\":\"image\",\"frames\":[1,2,3],\"mask\":\"part\",\"region\":[0,0,31,31],\"colors\":{\"#5a3a2a\":\"#22304a\",\"#8a5a40\":\"#34507a\",\"#c08a68\":\"#5078a8\"},\"reason\":\"Old ramp to new ramp, dark to light.\"}\n]\n```\n\n## Existing PNGs and GIFs\n\nEach command writes a **new** workspace directory. The flattened asset's layer name is `image`.\n\n```sh\npython tools/pixel_edit_job.py prepare data-test/archer.png --out experiments/my-repair/source\npython tools/pixel_edit_job.py inspect data-test/archer.png --frames 1 --region 25 9 51 34 --out experiments/my-repair/detail\npython tools/pixel_edit_job.py apply data-test/archer.png decision.json --out experiments/my-repair/pass-01\n```\n\nAn apply decision contains exactly `version: 1`, `source_sha256` copied from the source packet, and `changes`, a nonempty array of the operation objects above.\n\nInspect every source frame and palette before choosing edits. `inspect` supplies a 10x nearest-neighbor crop and `pixels.json`: RGBA colors/counts and a grid of palette indices when the crops total at most 4096 pixels. Grid dimensions are `[selected_frame][crop_y][crop_x]`; add the region's left/top for image coordinates. This supports guarded patches without guessing colors from a screenshot.\n\n`apply` exports `candidate.png` or `candidate.gif`, numeric `layers.npz`, an unchanged input copy, the decision, per-operation audit, all-frame source/candidate sheets, change masks, palettes and an HTML comparison with a shared clock. `packet.json` lists the images to actually open or attach. Swatches show up to 128 colors with RGBA labels; `palettes.json` contains every frame's complete palette. PNG alpha remains exact. GIF timing, loop setting, duplicate frames and visible pixels are decoded and checked after export. Partial-alpha GIF edits and lossy palette reduction are rejected.\n\nKeep the original and accumulated decisions. Replay the targeted operations against the original when possible; if a previous candidate becomes the next input, its hash must match the new decision. This tool reports `needs_visual_review`; it does not automatically accept artwork or enforce the full review loop.\n\n## Layered animation jobs\n\nThe same operations work in the [animation decision contract](ANIMATION_WORKFLOW.md) on `python-drawing` jobs. Add `view` to each operation and use a layer **name** from `packet.json`, such as `Body - source`. Put changes inside the existing `candidate_id` / `decision: \"edit\"` envelope. Mirrors follow their original view.\n\nRendering order: scene/node drawing, legacy `pixels`/`replace_color` edits, then the accumulated new raster-operation list. `copy_region` reads the rendered layers before that raster list, including poses and legacy edits. Later node changes can invalidate prior pixel guards; inspect and revise those patches. Source-protection and palette checks still run after all edits. `pixel-edits.json` records matched/changed counts, bounding boxes, alpha changes, additions and erasures; its hash is included in deliverable integrity checks.\n\nExact layer inspection:\n\n```json\n{\"candidate_id\":\"COPY_FROM_RESULT\",\"decision\":\"inspect\",\"inspections\":[{\"view\":\"south\",\"mode\":\"layer\",\"layer\":\"Body - source\",\"frames\":[3,4,5],\"region\":[30,35,45,50],\"scale\":10,\"reason\":\"Inspect grip colors and neighboring poses before editing pixels.\"}]}\n```\n\nThis returns PNG crops and `pixels.json` tied to that candidate. For new geometry or articulated poses, use [scene commands and `set_node`](DRAWING_SCENES.md).\n\n## Backend functions\n\n`server.pixel_edits.apply_edits(layers, changes, protected=None)` accepts named `uint8` arrays shaped `[frame,height,width,4]` and returns new arrays plus an audit. Optional **trusted** boolean protection masks are `[frame,height,width]` per layer; touching them rejects an operation. Keep protection policy in application configuration, outside model-controlled changes. `inspect_region(layers, layer, frames, region)` provides the same palette/grid inspection without file I/O.\n\n`tools.pixel_edit_job.build(source, output_directory, decision=None, scale=2)` handles PNG/GIF import, evidence and export. `tools.animation_job.decide(...)` handles layered animation revisions. These functions use NumPy and Pillow. The animation Docker definition includes them but has not been built or deployed here.\n\nReview every new candidate and affected neighboring frames. A smaller palette or passing tests does not prove an artistic improvement. This setup removes repeated code generation; image review and reasoning still use tokens. No per-asset price target has been established by these deterministic tests.\n\nValidation: `python -m unittest server.test_pixel_edits server.test_drawing_renderer too
"}

